add(26,'unproved claim','15/215:L486–498','avec la comp. conn. … engendre G; pleinement fidèle','For the stated real-base setting, real Frobenius is claimed to generate G together with G⁰, and adding Hodge and realification data is claimed fully faithful.','The component quotient must be generated by that involution; for arbitrary relative motives the geometric finite monodromy can add components. The intended category is not fully fixed.','The insertion changes geometrically connected to irreducible, not necessarily geometrically irreducible. The note retains that change and does not manufacture a general descent theorem.')
add(27,'conjecture','15/215:L507–509;15/217:L556–561','étudier comment varie la structure de Hodge','Recover the variation of Hodge structure from one fibre together with compatible monodromy.','For geometric polarizable variations over a connected algebraic base, fixed-part results control monodromy-invariant Hom sections; arbitrary analytic local systems are outside that statement.','The note identifies an applicable later mechanism rather than declaring all variations determined without hypotheses.')
add(28,'unproved claim','15/217:L556–561','déterminé … en un point, plus l’application π₁','A map to a moduli space of polarized rigidified motives is asserted determined by its value at one point and induced π₁ map.','A precise proven special case uses abelian schemes and the fixed-part theorem; general motivic moduli and rigidity require definition and fullness.','Both pieces of input are written. The note correctly limits the later theorem to a specified geometric setting.')
add(29,'conjecture','15/217:L563–569','Peut-être … toutes … variétés modulaires','Ask whether all varieties with the preceding rigidity property are moduli spaces for some types of motives.','The admissible varieties, morphisms and motives are unspecified; no universal modern equivalence is inferred.','The universal and tentative wording is visible. This inverse moduli problem is distinct from proving rigidity for a given moduli space.')
add(30,'unpursued implication','15/217:L573–579','schéma motiviquement pointé','A motivically pointed scheme is a scheme with an h-valued fibre functor; compatible pointed morphisms induce homomorphisms of tensor-automorphism groups and h-points.','A pointed morphism must include a tensor identification of the fibre functors; otherwise the map is only defined up to conjugation.','The construction is proposed explicitly. The note supplies the needed identification as reconstruction, not a quotation.')
add(31,'conjecture','15/219:L621–628','Quand … pour tte sous-catégorie … de t.f.','Compare global isomorphism of two integral fibre functors with separate isomorphism on every finitely generated tensor subcategory.','Global isomorphism requires a compatible point in the inverse system of Isom torsors, not merely nonemptiness of each stage.','The two questions are individually stated. The note correctly treats compatibility as a substantive issue.')
add(32,'unproved claim','15/219:L630–674','grâce au groupoïde fondamental transcendant','If archimedean places agree on the algebraic closure of Q in k, their restrictions to every finite tensor piece are asserted isomorphic by spreading to geometrically connected finite-type models. If they agree on k, a full isomorphism is asserted.','Descent of motives, all tensor relations and integral realizations to these models must be justified for a chosen category.','The page sketches the reduction and uses normality/geometric connectedness; it is not a completed category-independent proof.')
add(33,'unproved claim','15/221:L720–734','transition … surjectifs; lim … ≠∅','For countable k, a sequential system of smooth models with geometrically connected fibres is claimed to give surjective path-set transitions and hence a compatible path system.','The inverse-limit lemma is valid conditional on surjectivity. The tower itself cannot exist for k equal to an algebraic closure of Qbar(t): regular fraction-field transitions would force each finitely generated fraction field to be algebraically closed. This does not refute the final fibre-functor isomorphism.','The entry accurately records the claim but understates the failure of the asserted geometric construction; the independent obstruction is CL-SA07-134 and computations.md §D8.',check='understates',verdict='corrected')
add(34,'self-flagged step','15/221:L736','Si k pas dénombrable,','The uncountable-field extension of the preceding comparison is left unfinished.','No failure, compactness argument or transfinite construction can be inferred from this abandoned sentence.','The phrase has no continuation before the next numbered case.')
add(35,'conjecture','15/221:L738–746','je pense qu’il existe α','If two archimedean places differ on the algebraic part of the field, some finite tensor piece is expected to distinguish their integral Betti fibre functors.','This quantifies over prescribed pairs; a single pair of conjugate varieties with different rings is insufficient. Field symbols E|k remain uncertain.','The narrower expectation follows the retracted unrestricted claim and is not itself crossed out.')
add(36,'unpursued implication','15/223:L795–796','non isomorphes comme anneaux; Z par R??','A smooth projective conjugate pair with different graded integral, or even real, cohomology rings would distinguish compatible tensor fibre functors.','The inference uses preservation of the multiplication morphism of the cohomology algebra object. The existential real example does not give every prescribed pair of places.','The page explicitly seeks a ring witness and asks for real coefficients. Charles provides the later existential answer, checked separately.')
add(37,'conjecture','15/223:L799–802','si M est pris assez grand','For a number field, one sufficiently large motive is conjectured to make the map from archimedean places to integral tensor-Isom torsor classes injective.','Integral category and group scheme, and whether M is common to all places, require specification.','The page explicitly labels the preceding separation conjectural and conditions the injection on it.')
add(38,'conjecture','15/223:L803–804','∀ fibré principal homogène P … provient d’une place','The author asks whether every torsor under the chosen integral motivic group arises from a place, then warns that a complex comparison satisfying Riemann–Hodge conditions may be decisive.','The viable target is an as-yet undefined category of enriched admissible torsors; unadorned H¹(Z,G) is not automatically geometric.','The universal question and immediate restriction warning are both present, so no bare-torsor surjectivity theorem is inferred.')
add(39,'idea of the earlier run','15/223:L807–810','ces groupes à priori ne sont pas isomorphes','Different fibre-functor torsors need not yield different abstract groups of integral points.','This is a logical warning about forgetting tensors and group-scheme structure, not a specific arithmetic counterexample.','The page only says a priori; the earlier-run note does not overclaim a proved nonisomorphism.')
