add(66,'conjecture','18/013:L1184–1188','deg tr Q(α)=4 resp 2 ?','For an elliptic curve over Q̄, ask for full period-torsor transcendence degree four without CM and two with CM.','The intended torsor includes periods and quasi-periods; the CM case is established, the full non-CM equality remains beyond the checked dRB-torsor theorem.','The live page explicitly separates the two groups GL₂ and Res_{K/Q}G_m and the two predicted dimensions.')
add(67,'conjecture','18/013:L1189–1193','A-t-on H=G','After translating the Q̄-Zariski locus of an elliptic comparison point to contain the identity, ask whether its generated algebraic subgroup is the full motivic group.','Its identification with the minimal algebraic de Rham–Betti torsor uses a torsor argument. KSV Proposition 3.1 and Theorem 5.13(ii) supply the precise later affirmative statement for one elliptic curve.','The question is genuinely weaker than genericity of the period point. The note’s later comparison preserves this distinction.')
add(68,'conjecture','18/013:L1194–1195','Peut-on avoir h∈Q̄','For a non-CM elliptic curve over Q̄, ask whether the analytic Hodge splitting of its de Rham realization can be algebraic.','Katz’s Question 4.0.8 asks the equivalent converse after descending the curve, splitting and endomorphism data to a number field. The checked sources do not prove its resolution.','The algebraic structure is de Rham, not the rational Betti structure. The note matches Katz’s question without claiming priority or a theorem.')
add(69,'unproved claim','18/013:L1196','si C est à multiplication complexe … h∈Q̄ !','A CM elliptic curve’s analytic Hodge splitting is defined over an algebraic extension containing its endomorphisms and their eigenvalues.','A non-scalar CM endomorphism gives two distinct algebraic de Rham eigenlines; Katz Lemma 4.0.7 identifies them with the Hodge summands.','The margin asserts exactly the CM direction, and the independent eigenspace argument verifies it with the field-extension qualification.')
add(70,'reading question','18/012:L1160–1164;18/012:L1172–1179','pré-minimal|pré-maximal; ζ|ξ','The cancelled normalized conjecture has an uncertain base-field condition and an undefined normalizing symbol.','The Legendre determinant explains a possible Tate normalization, but cannot determine the symbol, its square root or the base-field definition.','The note correctly leaves these as genuine reading/definition gaps instead of reconstructing a unique theorem.')
add(71,'unproved claim','18/015:L1207–1225;18/017:L1262–1275','P_top=(2iπ)⁻ᵖ P_an; ∫ dz/z=2iπ','For compatible local complex orientations, the analytic algebraic cycle class equals (2πi)^p times the topological class in codimension p.','The local normal-crossing residue computation is explicit; its global use requires support, cup-product and functoriality compatibilities.','The scalar and one-dimensional integral are written, while multiplicativity is introduced conditionally. The note correctly separates the local computation and globalization.')
add(72,'reading question','18/015:L1220–1222','X′^{n−p} transversale','A transversal cutting a codimension-p subvariety of an n-dimensional variety in isolated points should have dimension p.','n−p may reflect an unstated convention or a draft slip; no reading is changed.','The isolated-point argument and written dimension conflict under the ordinary convention.')
add(73,'reading question','18/017:L1280–1283','H^{i+p}; Il est commutatif','The global codimension-p Gysin comparison diagram displays a degree shift i+p, while the local diagram uses 2p.','Ordinary singular and de Rham cohomology require i+2p; a regrading could only be used if explicitly defined. Support indices are also overwritten.','The note correctly identifies a cohomological-degree mismatch and keeps the transcription unchanged.')
add(74,'self-flagged step','18/015:L1221–1225;18/017:L1260;18/017:L1283','faire le raisonnement; Provient de la comm. de','The author requests a final argument for local-to-global cycle-class comparison and ends the explanation of the global square unfinished.','A proof must establish compatibility with local orientations, products, support maps and gluing, not merely the value of a circle integral.','The marginal request and unfinished last sentence are explicit. The note correctly indexes a proof gap distinct from the scalar computation.')
add(75,'unpursued implication','18/009:L1050–1057;18/011:L1131;18/017:L1268–1275','même … module sur Z; (2iπ)^n','An abstract integral lattice may stay the same under the manuscript’s filtration shift, while its comparison embedding acquires the Tate period (2πi)^n.','The lattice Q(n) or Z(n), its Hodge indices, and the analytic comparison embedding must be recorded as different data.','The three passages are consistent only with this distinction, and the note states it accurately.')
add(76,'unproved claim','18/006:L989–1008','On écrit que σ̃₁(γ)=σ̃₂(γ)','Centralization makes each modified section a homomorphism, but their asserted equality is an additional compatibility between the two realizations.','Two sections of one extension may differ by a nontrivial cocycle; equality requires identifying their common origin or an explicit comparison condition.','The equality is introduced separately and is not implied by the preceding centralization calculation. The note correctly exposes the extra step.')
add(77,'unproved claim','18/006:L981–982;18/006:L1020–1022','C’est un groupe analytique p-adique','If π is profinite and u is continuous into a finite-dimensional Q_p-linear algebraic group, then π/ker u≅u(π) is compact, closed, and p-adic analytic.','Finite-dimensionality and continuity are essential; an unrestricted proalgebraic target does not give this conclusion.','The note supplies the standard closed-subgroup argument with its missing hypotheses, rather than deriving analyticity from compactness alone.')
add(78,'unpursued implication','15/219:L621–628;18/011:L1081–1088','sous-catégorie … de t.f. de M(X)','These sheets use a motivic category, automorphism groups and comparison functors; they do not construct the category from the Lefschetz operator rings.','The usual pure-motive construction additionally requires correspondences, an equivalence relation, composition, tensor structure, idempotent completion and twists.','The note correctly distinguishes category-level structure from one operator algebra and retains the missing cote14 construction as an external lead.')
add(79,'idea of the earlier run','18/012:L1172–1177;18/013:L1184','courbe elliptique sans mult. complexe','The nontrivial SL₂ in the normalized elliptic period problem is not the Lefschetz sl₂ action on H¹(E).','For a smooth proper curve both L:H¹→H³ and Λ:H¹→H⁻¹ are zero; the conclusion is an earlier-run degree-range test.','The test is decisive and does not depend on the unclear superscript on G in the cancelled draft.')
